1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:01,900
It is the second week of March,

2
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:07,060
2025, two of the world's leading squid scientists have just come off a ship and they

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:11,900
have failed. For five weeks they had been lowering a camera into the southern ocean,

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,760
a rig built for one purpose.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,160
To film a full -grown colossal squid,

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,120
the heaviest invertebrate on Earth.

7
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,320
Nobody had ever done it, not once in a hundred years.

8
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,580
They didn't do it either,

9
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,960
the gear was packed, they had said their goodbyes to everybody.

10
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:34,190
And then, in a rented flat in southern Argentina,

11
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:35,850
Kat Bolstad opened a file.

12
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:39,270
In her own words, there was a moment,

13
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:41,250
it was a very specific moment.

14
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,930
A low resolution clip, something pale,

15
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:49,790
drifting at 600 metras filmed the day before by somebody else's robot on a different

16
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:52,530
ocean. She could not be sure.

17
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,910
In this family of squid, almost everything looks like almost everything else.

18
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:00,230
So she asked for the high resolution version,

19
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:01,850
and she went looking for hooks,

20
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,600
suckers near the beak, then hooks midway along the arm,

21
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:09,880
then suckers again toward the tips,

22
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,380
nothing else on Earth is built that way.

23
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:17,390
And I was like, yeah, they've got it.

24
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:21,170
Her colleague Erin Evans put it more plainly,

25
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:23,170
I started hyperventilating.

26
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:26,720
After 100 years of corpses,

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,560
we finally had one alive.

28
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:32,040
It was 30 centimeters long,

29
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:36,060
it was a baby, and the adult,

30
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,640
the 495 kilogram animal this species is famous for,

31
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:41,760
has still never been seen alive by anyone.

32
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:47,420
This is the story of an animal we named a hundred years ago,

33
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:50,600
from two crowns of arms cut out of a sperm whale's stomach,

34
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:54,180
and of the 100 years we spent never seeing a grown one.

35
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:59,130
Chapter 1, What the Whale Was Carrying.

36
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:04,410
Start with the thing that makes this animal different from every other famous creature

37
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:06,550
in the sea. We did not find it,

38
00:02:06,910 --> 00:02:08,250
we found it inside something else.

39
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,780
The winter of 1924, South Georgia,

40
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:15,220
in the far south Atlantic,

41
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:17,640
is the center of the world's whaling industry.

42
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,940
Men are cutting open sperm whales on an industrial scale,

43
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:26,040
and inside the stomachs, again and again,

44
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,560
they are finding parts of something they cannot name.

45
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:33,350
Two of those parts reach the British Museum in London.

46
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:37,900
Not tentacles, not beaks.

47
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,800
Two crowns of arms, from two different animals,

48
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,560
cut out of a sperm whale's stomach.

49
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:50,370
A zoologist named Guy Coburn -Robson describes them in 1925 in the Annals and Magazine

50
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:55,640
of Natural History. He gives it a genus of its own,

51
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:57,960
mesonicotuthis. It means,

52
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,000
roughly, middle -claw squid.

53
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,880
Remember that, it will matter a hundred years later.

54
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:07,840
The species name honors a man called Hamilton.

55
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:10,280
Almost certainly James Eric Hamilton,

56
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:12,600
the Falkland Islands government naturalist,

57
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:16,780
who spent whaling seasons living on Deception Island and shipping specimens back to London.

58
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:22,750
Almost certainly, Robson's own paper is not something we could read.

59
00:03:23,130 --> 00:03:24,790
There is no open scan of it anywhere.

60
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:29,400
We are not even certain where the whale was killed.

61
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:33,860
One source says 50 miles north of Smith Island in the South Shetlands.

62
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:35,560
Another says the Falklands.

63
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:38,020
Those are 1 ,500 kilometers apart.

64
00:03:40,590 --> 00:03:42,850
The likeliest answer is boring and human.

65
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:47,890
In 1925, the South Shetlands were administered as the Falkland Islands dependencies.

66
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:49,610
Somewhere along the line,

67
00:03:49,990 --> 00:03:51,410
a filing label became a location.

68
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:56,960
That is the founding record of the largest invertebrate on the planet.

69
00:03:57,580 --> 00:03:59,940
Two sets of arms, an uncertain map reference,

70
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:02,080
and a paper almost nobody has read.

71
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:07,380
Chapter two, how many have we actually found?

72
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,060
Here's a question that sounds simple.

73
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:13,940
How many colossal squid have humans ever found?

74
00:04:16,570 --> 00:04:21,410
To Papa, the museum in New Zealand that holds the most famous specimen says eight adults,

75
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:23,970
six of them out of sperm whale stomachs.

76
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:29,810
A 2015 audit of ocean giants counts 12 complete specimens,

77
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,290
half of them juveniles, plus 55 records that are nothing but a beak.

78
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,560
A review of the species counts about 155 animals caught in nets.

79
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:43,760
Another paper counts 188 positions on a map.

80
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:47,850
Every one of those numbers is correct.

81
00:04:48,410 --> 00:04:49,650
They are counting different things.

82
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:52,590
Bodies, arm crowns, beaks.

83
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:56,680
And the beaks multiply fast.

84
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:01,600
One study of sleeper sharks off the Kirkland Islands pulled 89 beaks out of 22 sharks,

85
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:03,960
at least 49 individual squid.

86
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:08,020
One paper, one shark's stomach at a time,

87
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:11,500
out counting every intact colossal squid ever recovered.

88
00:05:14,170 --> 00:05:15,730
So the honest answer is a range.

89
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:19,050
Somewhere between eight and a dozen complete or adult animals,

90
00:05:19,330 --> 00:05:21,570
against roughly 150 netted juveniles,

91
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:24,330
and hundreds of beaks pulled out of things that ate them.

92
00:05:25,610 --> 00:05:27,210
The largest invertebrate on Earth.

93
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:31,490
In 100 years, we have collected fewer whole adults than you have fingers.

94
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:36,730
Chapter three, the ones we never caught.

95
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:40,820
And there is a pattern in the ones we did get,

96
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:43,280
which nobody planned and everybody noticed.

97
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:50,200
Between 1962 and 1985, research nets brought up 33 young colossal squid.

98
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,700
Their mantles ran from 3 millimeters to 146.

99
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:59,200
The smallest whole animal on record is a larva 23 millimeters long.

100
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:00,860
You could lose it in your hand.

101
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,200
Nets catch babies. Nets almost never catch adults.

102
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:11,020
Nearly every large colossal squid we have ever held came up on a fishing line or came

103
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,060
out of a predator's stomach.

104
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,940
Not one was found because somebody went looking for it.

105
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:19,340
And there is one more thing in the record that is genuinely strange.

106
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:24,860
Of the large specimens, essentially all of them are female.

107
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:27,440
A mature male appears exactly once,

108
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,360
among partial remains at the Smithsonian.

109
00:06:32,150 --> 00:06:35,970
We have never properly seen a grown male of the heaviest invertebrate on the planet.

110
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:38,470
We do not know where they are.

111
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,200
Chapter four, the squid that shrank six metros.

112
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,800
February 2007, the Ross Sea,

113
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:54,000
Antarctica. A New Zealand longliner called the San Aspiring is fishing for Antarctic

114
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,240
tooth fish. 1500 metros down,

115
00:06:57,760 --> 00:07:00,220
something takes hold of a tooth fish that is already on the hook.

116
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:05,180
And it will not let go. It rides the line all the way to the surface.

117
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:08,920
Nine men, roughly two hours.

118
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:12,880
In Antarctic water, they get it aboard with a crane and a cargo net.

119
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:17,360
It is the first mature colossal squid ever recovered intact.

120
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:22,320
It is, still today, the heaviest cephalopod anyone has ever documented.

121
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:27,880
At sea, they estimate it at eight to ten metros long.

122
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:33,040
It is gifted to Te Papa, the National Museum in Wellington,

123
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:36,500
and frozen. It stays frozen for 14 months.

124
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:42,500
In April 2008, they thawed out and measure it properly,

125
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:47,360
4 .2 metros. The same animal,

126
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:52,330
six metros shorter. Nobody lied.

127
00:07:53,150 --> 00:07:54,810
Squid tentacles shorten and shrink after death.

128
00:07:55,490 --> 00:07:58,430
Commercial squid tissue can lose up to 22 % to dehydration,

129
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:00,750
and this one spent 14 months in a freezer.

130
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:08,200
Which means the 10 -metra monster in the 2007 headlines and the four -metra animal in

131
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:10,200
the case in Wellington are the same creature.

132
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:15,980
Both numbers are honest. This is the first thing to understand

133
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:17,060
about the colossal squid.

134
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,640
Almost every number attached to it is softer than it sounds.

135
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:25,400
Chapter five, length is a lie.

136
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:30,100
Weight is where this animal is genuinely extraordinary.

137
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:34,000
Fully thawed, that squid weighed 495 kilograms.

138
00:08:36,710 --> 00:08:38,450
Though even there, be careful.

139
00:08:39,310 --> 00:08:43,790
Te Papa's own pages give about 470 kilograms is the estimate for the animal in life,

140
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:48,860
and 495 with ice. Length,

141
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:51,440
though, is close to useless for a squid.

142
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:54,080
Look at what happens when you compare two specimens.

143
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:58,040
An animal recovered in 2003 was longer,

144
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:01,940
5 .4 metros. It weighed 300 kilograms.

145
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,280
The 2007 animal was shorter,

146
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:09,360
at 4 .2 metros, and weighed nearly 200 kilograms more.

147
00:09:12,030 --> 00:09:15,210
Te Papa's phrase for this is that they are incredibly plastic animals.

148
00:09:15,890 --> 00:09:20,050
Total length depends on how far the tentacles happen to be stretched and what death did

149
00:09:20,050 --> 00:09:24,880
to them. So scientists use mantle length instead.

150
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:30,520
The body, not the arms. And the largest mantle ever measured on this species is 2

151
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:35,220
.5 metros. Which brings us to the number you have almost certainly heard,

152
00:09:35,660 --> 00:09:39,100
and which is wrong. 12 to 14 metros.

153
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:41,900
It is everywhere. It has never been measured,

154
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:44,240
and it has never been credibly extrapolated.

155
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:48,380
It appears to be a straight confusion with the giant squid,

156
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:50,580
which is a genuinely longer animal.

157
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:53,020
And that comparison is worth making properly,

158
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:55,020
because the two get mixed up constantly.

159
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:00,560
The giant squid has been verified to 12 metros long.

160
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:04,020
But the heaviest complete one is around 220 kilograms,

161
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:06,720
and 95 % come in under 250.

162
00:10:09,630 --> 00:10:14,770
The often quoted 900 kilogram giant squid traces back to an estimate from 1879,

163
00:10:15,130 --> 00:10:17,070
which modern reviewers judge unreliable.

164
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:22,460
So longest squid, the giant.

165
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:24,560
Heaviest squid, the colossal.

166
00:10:25,020 --> 00:10:26,840
The giant squid is a longer animal.

167
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:28,960
The colossal squid is a bigger one.

168
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,380
Chapter six, the ruler is a beak.

169
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:38,180
Here's the strangest thing about how we measure this animal.

170
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:41,280
Most of the time, we are not measuring the animal.

171
00:10:43,910 --> 00:10:45,330
A squid's beak is made of chitin.

172
00:10:45,750 --> 00:10:50,490
It does not digest. When everything else about a colossal squid dissolves inside a whale,

173
00:10:50,970 --> 00:10:56,200
the beak survives. And the colossal squid has the largest

174
00:10:56,200 --> 00:10:57,840
beak of any living cephalopod,

175
00:10:58,380 --> 00:11:00,920
roughly twice the size of a giant squid's.

176
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:04,980
Beak size scales with body size.

177
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:08,600
So you can take a black parrot beak out of a whale's stomach and calculate backwards

178
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,040
to an animal nobody ever saw.

179
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:16,980
The 495 kilogram specimen had a lower beak around 42 millimeters.

180
00:11:19,710 --> 00:11:22,630
The largest beak ever pulled out of a sperm whale measures 49.

181
00:11:25,490 --> 00:11:28,710
To Papa's estimate for what that animal might have weighed is 600,

182
00:11:28,710 --> 00:11:33,740
maybe 700 kilograms. And the museum

183
00:11:33,740 --> 00:11:35,480
says, in the same breath,

184
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:40,780
that they cannot say for sure what size of colossal squid a 49 millimeter beak represents.

185
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:44,200
There are too few complete animals to build the equation properly.

186
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:49,460
What that beak does not mean is a 14 -metra animal.

187
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:54,940
The 2007 squid carried a 42 millimeter beak on a 2 .5 -metra body.

188
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:58,000
So somewhere out there, probably,

189
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:00,840
is a colossal squid heavier than any we have weighed.

190
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:05,400
It is not a sea monster. It is a slightly bigger version of the one in the case.

191
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:10,920
Chapter seven, a science built on dead whales,

192
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:14,780
which raises an uncomfortable question.

193
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:16,840
Where did all these beaks come from?

194
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:24,010
In 1980, a researcher named Malcolm Clark published an analysis of what sperm whales

195
00:12:24,010 --> 00:12:25,630
in the southern hemisphere had been eating.

196
00:12:28,220 --> 00:12:33,220
By his reconstruction, colossal squid beaks were about 14 % of the beaks found in Antarctic

197
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:38,360
sperm whale stomachs. But because the animal is so

198
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:43,540
heavy, that 14 % of beaks represents an estimated 77 % of everything

199
00:12:43,540 --> 00:12:48,010
those whales had eaten. Be careful with that figure.

200
00:12:48,570 --> 00:12:50,490
It is a reconstruction from beak sizes,

201
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:52,190
not a weighing of stomachs.

202
00:12:52,390 --> 00:12:54,610
It comes from Antarctic feeding grounds specifically.

203
00:12:55,130 --> 00:12:56,370
It is not a global number.

204
00:12:58,970 --> 00:13:01,650
And there is the part that is genuinely hard to sit with.

205
00:13:04,310 --> 00:13:06,570
Clark published in a journal called Discovery Reports.

206
00:13:07,430 --> 00:13:10,730
Discovery Reports was the publication series of the Discovery Investigations,

207
00:13:10,730 --> 00:13:14,130
the Southern Ocean Survey that existed to serve the whaling industry.

208
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:21,140
Almost everything we know about the largest invertebrate on earth is a byproduct of industrially

209
00:13:21,140 --> 00:13:26,460
killing sperm whales. 17 different predators

210
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:27,980
are known to eat them. Penguins,

211
00:13:28,500 --> 00:13:30,620
seabirds, fish, marine mammals.

212
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:36,260
At the Kergalin Islands, colossal squid remains turned up and around two -thirds of the

213
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:40,380
sleeper sharks examined. Albatrosses carry the remains too.

214
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,320
Though more than 60 % of what they carry had probably been scavenged.

215
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:47,460
They are eating dead colossal squid off the surface,

216
00:13:47,860 --> 00:13:51,240
not catching them. One thing to be careful about,

217
00:13:51,540 --> 00:13:53,440
because documentaries get it wrong constantly.

218
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:57,880
Those famous circular scars on sperm whales are not evidence of colossal squid.

219
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:02,820
Ring scars come from toothed sucker rings,

220
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,600
which is giant squid equipment.

221
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,040
The colossal squid leaves a different mark.

222
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:08,400
It leaves hook scratches.

223
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:13,930
And we do have those on the bodies of captured tooth fish.

224
00:14:14,690 --> 00:14:16,870
Bite marks and hook scratches that match this species.

225
00:14:16,870 --> 00:14:22,070
That is real. Published species matched evidence of a colossal squid attacking something

226
00:14:22,070 --> 00:14:26,810
large. Chapter eight, the thaw live on camera.

227
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:31,600
2014, the sand aspiring again.

228
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:35,920
The raw sea again. Another colossal squid comes up on a tooth fish line.

229
00:14:37,760 --> 00:14:39,860
Female, around 350 kilograms,

230
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:42,600
three and a half metros, recovered whole,

231
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:44,320
except that it's tentacles are missing.

232
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:48,740
And there are eggs inside the mantle.

233
00:14:51,430 --> 00:14:52,880
On the 16th of September,

234
00:14:53,320 --> 00:14:54,520
they thaw it in front of the world.

235
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:56,920
Cat Bolstad leads the examination.

236
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,720
It is streamed live on the internet for about three and a half hours.

237
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:08,600
Topapa reports more than 800 ,000 people watching live from over 180 countries.

238
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:11,560
A museum dissection with a global audience.

239
00:15:14,390 --> 00:15:16,830
Working alongside her, counting suckers on the arms,

240
00:15:17,030 --> 00:15:19,610
is a postgraduate researcher named Aaron Boyd Evans.

241
00:15:22,470 --> 00:15:24,770
Remember him. 11 years later,

242
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:28,710
he is one of the two people the world will ask to confirm the footage from the Airbnb.

243
00:15:31,670 --> 00:15:34,190
Chapter nine, the eye that watches for whales.

244
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:40,360
Now the animal. And the place to start is the part of it that is genuinely,

245
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:42,840
measurably unlike anything else alive.

246
00:15:45,540 --> 00:15:48,820
The colossal squid has the largest eye of any living animal on earth.

247
00:15:51,530 --> 00:15:53,350
How large depends on who measured it.

248
00:15:53,570 --> 00:15:56,510
And that difference is worth understanding rather than smoothing over.

249
00:15:59,050 --> 00:16:02,790
The figure that survives peer review is about 27 centimeters across,

250
00:16:03,270 --> 00:16:04,510
with a pupil around nine.

251
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,200
Topapa measured about 30 on their specimen.

252
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:13,880
On the 2014 animal, they recorded 35 to 37.

253
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:18,080
All of those can be true at once.

254
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:21,640
A squid's eye is a fluid -filled sphere with no bone around it.

255
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:23,440
After death, it flattens,

256
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:27,820
swells, and slumps. Different teams were measuring differently damaged things.

257
00:16:30,410 --> 00:16:34,310
For scale, the largest fish eyes on earth reach about nine or ten centimeters.

258
00:16:35,150 --> 00:16:38,430
This is roughly three times the diameter of any other living animal's eye.

259
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:44,020
One caution, you will hear largest eye of any animal ever.

260
00:16:44,520 --> 00:16:48,960
That is not safe. Reconstructions of a Jurassic marine reptile called Temnodonosaurus

261
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:52,280
give eyeballs around 26 centimeters and possibly over 30.

262
00:16:54,940 --> 00:16:56,920
So why would an animal grow an eye like that?

263
00:16:57,100 --> 00:17:00,000
And this is where the story stops being settled and starts being an argument.

264
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:06,240
In 2012, a team led by Dan Eric Nilsson at Lund University published a

265
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:07,860
hypothesis in current biology.

266
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:10,740
Their argument starts with a problem.

267
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:14,380
In water, making an eye bigger buys you less and less.

268
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:16,820
Absorption and scattering eat the gains.

269
00:17:18,320 --> 00:17:20,580
By their numbers, at 300 metros,

270
00:17:20,940 --> 00:17:23,400
growing a one -millimeter eye by 10 % buys,

271
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:27,500
about 6 .5 % more range. Do the same to a 100 -millimeter eye,

272
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:32,200
and you gain 2 .5%. A squid eye is three times bigger than that.

273
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:35,440
It is an enormous biological expense for almost nothing.

274
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:40,660
Unless what you are looking for is not small and nearby,

275
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:46,180
but huge and rare. Because the volume of water you can monitor grows as the cube of your

276
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:50,460
range. And here is the part almost every retelling gets wrong.

277
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:52,700
The squid is not looking for the whale.

278
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:57,260
Below about 600 metros, there is effectively no daylight.

279
00:17:57,680 --> 00:18:01,680
The only large glowing things down there are animals setting off the plankton as they

280
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:06,900
move through it. A sperm whale diving hard displaces a cylinder of water and lights

281
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:09,060
itself up. A diffuse glow,

282
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:14,700
whale -shaped, moving. The squid is looking for the light the whale knocks out of

283
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:18,590
the water. Nilsson's team modeled it.

284
00:18:18,930 --> 00:18:21,110
At 800 metros, in clear water,

285
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:25,830
a whale -sized glowing object should be detectable at nearly 120 metros.

286
00:18:25,830 --> 00:18:28,530
Almost twice the range for anything else visible.

287
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:33,580
And then the detail that makes it sadder and better than the headline version.

288
00:18:36,030 --> 00:18:39,310
A sperm whale's echolocation reaches further than 120 metros.

289
00:18:39,910 --> 00:18:41,610
The squid does not win this race.

290
00:18:42,290 --> 00:18:44,030
The whale has already heard it.

291
00:18:46,120 --> 00:18:48,280
The most the eye buys, on this hypothesis,

292
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:51,260
is a little warning. Enough time to try to run.

293
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:56,040
That is the theory. Now the argument.

294
00:18:57,340 --> 00:19:02,640
In 2013, a group led by Lars Schmitz compared eyes across 87 squid species

295
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,740
and more than 200 fish species.

296
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:08,480
Their conclusion. Relative to body size,

297
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:10,500
these eyes are not exceptional at all.

298
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,160
They are about what you would predict for a squid that big.

299
00:19:14,600 --> 00:19:17,880
On their reading, spotting whales might well be something the eye can do.

300
00:19:18,420 --> 00:19:20,140
But it is a side effect of being enormous,

301
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:21,840
not the reason the eye exists.

302
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:26,800
Nilsson's team replied, they accepted Schmitz's revised numbers,

303
00:19:27,020 --> 00:19:28,620
called them more appropriately conservative,

304
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,380
ran the model again with them,

305
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:34,720
and got the same answer. Schmitz's group replied to the reply,

306
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:37,460
it has not been settled. It is,

307
00:19:37,620 --> 00:19:41,580
as of today, still an open argument between two groups of serious people.

308
00:19:43,910 --> 00:19:45,370
Which is worth saying out loud,

309
00:19:45,650 --> 00:19:50,310
because you will find 100 videos telling you flatly that this animal evolved giant eyes

310
00:19:50,310 --> 00:19:51,570
to see sperm whales coming.

311
00:19:52,070 --> 00:19:53,470
Nobody actually knows that.

312
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:58,820
There is one more thing on those eyes that almost never gets mentioned.

313
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:01,700
Each one carries two light organs of its own.

314
00:20:04,410 --> 00:20:06,250
What they are for is genuinely unknown.

315
00:20:06,850 --> 00:20:08,210
They might erase the eye's silhouette.

316
00:20:08,630 --> 00:20:09,470
They might be a deception.

317
00:20:10,010 --> 00:20:10,810
They might be headlights,

318
00:20:11,210 --> 00:20:13,130
or a way of judging the distance to strike.

319
00:20:13,690 --> 00:20:15,570
Three hypotheses, no test.

320
00:20:17,190 --> 00:20:19,350
Chapter 10. 25 hooks that spin.

321
00:20:21,700 --> 00:20:26,160
Now the weapons. And this is the part that makes this animal genuinely strange within

322
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:31,280
its own family. The colossal squid belongs to the glass

323
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:34,520
squids, Cranchiidae. Around 20 species,

324
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:37,120
most of them small, most of them transparent,

325
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:41,300
most of them harmless. It is the only one with hooks.

326
00:20:43,980 --> 00:20:46,200
And it carries two completely different kinds,

327
00:20:46,700 --> 00:20:48,380
which is a distinction worth getting right,

328
00:20:48,680 --> 00:20:50,480
because it decides the end of this story.

329
00:20:53,160 --> 00:20:55,080
The two long tentacles are the strike weapon.

330
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:58,600
Around two metras each. At the end of each is a club,

331
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:02,000
and on that club sit 22 to 25 hooks in two rows.

332
00:21:04,770 --> 00:21:07,290
Each hook sits on a short stalk in a shallow depression,

333
00:21:07,870 --> 00:21:10,550
and each one rotates, 360 degrees,

334
00:21:11,130 --> 00:21:12,410
by the museum's own account,

335
00:21:12,710 --> 00:21:17,620
up to 720. The eight arms are different,

336
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:21,060
shorter, around a metro. And their hooks are bigger,

337
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:23,380
fixed, and do not rotate at all.

338
00:21:26,140 --> 00:21:27,920
Most of the arm hooks are three -pointed,

339
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:35,040
a main claw, and two smaller cusps at the The

340
00:21:35,040 --> 00:21:38,920
likeliest way it works, and this is inference from anatomy rather than observation.

341
00:21:39,760 --> 00:21:41,840
The tentacles fire, the club hits,

342
00:21:42,440 --> 00:21:44,600
the spinning hooks catch at whatever angle they land,

343
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:45,940
and as the prey struggles,

344
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:48,040
they lock from several directions at once.

345
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:52,500
Then the prey gets drawn back to the arms,

346
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:56,780
where the fixed three -pointed hooks and the serrated suckers hold it still for the beak.

347
00:21:59,370 --> 00:22:01,850
This is grappling gear. It is not chasing gear.

348
00:22:02,270 --> 00:22:04,570
It is mechanical advantage standing in for speed.

349
00:22:04,570 --> 00:22:09,730
And there is something the museum is admirably honest about.

350
00:22:10,470 --> 00:22:13,150
Nobody knows whether the squid controls each of those spinning hooks,

351
00:22:13,650 --> 00:22:16,250
or whether they simply swivel on their own once something is caught.

352
00:22:19,070 --> 00:22:20,550
Nobody has ever watched it happen.

353
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:26,300
For contrast, the giant squid has no hooks at all.

354
00:22:26,740 --> 00:22:28,400
It grips with toothed sucker rings,

355
00:22:28,920 --> 00:22:32,680
two enormous animals, two completely different solutions to holding on.

356
00:22:35,270 --> 00:22:37,550
Chapter 11, 30 grams a day.

357
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:44,600
So you have an animal weighing half a ton with the largest eyes on Earth and 25 rotating

358
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:46,240
hooks. You would expect a monster.

359
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:52,900
In 2010, Rui Rosa and Brad Siebel modeled its metabolism.

360
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:58,540
Their estimate for a full -grown colossal squid in water at 1 .5 degrees,

361
00:22:59,180 --> 00:23:01,100
about 45 kilocalories a day,

362
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:04,860
which works out at roughly 30 grams of food,

363
00:23:05,360 --> 00:23:09,100
a day, for an animal that weighs as much as a grand piano.

364
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,800
One 5 -kilogrammed toothfish would keep it going for months.

365
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:17,760
Their conclusion, in their own words,

366
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:22,020
It is not a voracious predator capable of high -speed interactions.

367
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:24,840
It is an ambush, or sit -and -float,

368
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:29,480
predator that uses the hooks on its arms and tentacles to ensnare prey that unwittingly

369
00:23:29,480 --> 00:23:33,780
approach. And now the caveat that has to be said out loud,

370
00:23:34,140 --> 00:23:36,360
because it applies to nearly everything in this film.

371
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:40,520
Nobody has ever measured a colossal squid's oxygen consumption.

372
00:23:41,260 --> 00:23:42,920
Nobody has ever had a live one to measure.

373
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:47,420
That figure is a phylogenetic inference.

374
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:50,620
It is built from measurements of its smaller relatives,

375
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:53,860
scaled up by body size at southern ocean temperatures.

376
00:23:54,540 --> 00:23:56,640
It is the best estimate anyone has.

377
00:23:57,080 --> 00:24:01,660
It is not a measurement. The press turned that study into a lazy,

378
00:24:02,020 --> 00:24:05,360
harmless drifter, and the scientists pushed back on their own result,

379
00:24:05,760 --> 00:24:08,200
in print. Their question,

380
00:24:08,940 --> 00:24:12,160
does an organism need to be an active pursuit predator to be fearsome?

381
00:24:12,900 --> 00:24:15,080
White sharks, leopards, crocodiles,

382
00:24:15,240 --> 00:24:17,140
and pythons are all ambush predators,

383
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:19,060
many with low energy demands.

384
00:24:19,420 --> 00:24:23,180
It is their capability to hide and strike without warning that makes them dangerous.

385
00:24:24,620 --> 00:24:25,900
And the chemistry backs them.

386
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:30,880
Analysis of nitrogen isotopes places the colossal squid among the top predators in the

387
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:34,560
entire southern ocean. So not a monster,

388
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:37,160
and not a drifter. A slow,

389
00:24:37,560 --> 00:24:41,780
cold, cheap -to -run ambush animal that happens to sit at the top of one of the largest

390
00:24:41,780 --> 00:24:43,020
ecosystems on the planet.

391
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:47,760
Chapter 12. It breathes without swimming.

392
00:24:49,910 --> 00:24:50,970
One more piece of engineering,

393
00:24:51,430 --> 00:24:54,030
because it explains how something that big does so little.

394
00:24:55,610 --> 00:24:59,710
Glass squids carry an enormous internal chamber filled with a solution of ammonium chloride.

395
00:24:59,710 --> 00:25:02,090
It is about as salty as seawater,

396
00:25:02,230 --> 00:25:05,130
but lighter, and it cancels out the weight of the animal's tissue.

397
00:25:06,630 --> 00:25:09,290
Every other ammonia -floating squid spreads it through the muscle.

398
00:25:09,910 --> 00:25:12,090
The glass squids keep it in a dedicated tank.

399
00:25:13,490 --> 00:25:16,930
Which means they can hang in the water and breathe without ever contracting the mantle.

400
00:25:17,510 --> 00:25:19,790
They can float and respire without swimming at all.

401
00:25:21,930 --> 00:25:25,290
Whether that is fully true of a 495 -kilogram adult,

402
00:25:25,710 --> 00:25:30,590
nobody knows. The review that describes it flags the colossal squid as the family's odd

403
00:25:30,590 --> 00:25:33,310
one out, with far more muscle than its transparent cousins.

404
00:25:33,990 --> 00:25:36,510
No one has ever measured the ammonium content of an adult.

405
00:25:39,130 --> 00:25:41,550
And there is a grim little consequence to all that ammonia.

406
00:25:42,110 --> 00:25:45,390
It is part of why the tissue dissolves so fast inside a whale,

407
00:25:45,790 --> 00:25:47,250
leaving only the beaks and the hooks.

408
00:25:50,010 --> 00:25:53,350
Which is exactly why our record of the largest invertebrate on Earth is,

409
00:25:53,830 --> 00:25:55,610
mostly, a record of its mouthparts.

410
00:25:58,910 --> 00:26:01,390
Chapter 13. The dive that wasn't looking.

411
00:26:03,380 --> 00:26:07,000
The 9th of March, 2025. The South Sandwich Islands,

412
00:26:07,420 --> 00:26:11,400
in the South Atlantic. A research vessel called the Falcor,

413
00:26:12,040 --> 00:26:15,080
II, is 35 days into an expedition named Go South.

414
00:26:15,920 --> 00:26:18,680
Chief scientist, Michelle Taylor of the University of Essex.

415
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:21,780
Their remotely operated vehicle,

416
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:24,760
Sebastian, is descending to work on the seafloor.

417
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:27,860
It is not hunting squid. It is passing through.

418
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:33,340
At 600 metros, something small and transparent drifts through the lights.

419
00:26:34,900 --> 00:26:37,380
And here is the detail I love most in this entire story.

420
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:39,980
The person who first flagged it was not on the ship.

421
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:44,920
The Falcor, II, streams its dives publicly.

422
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:47,560
Somebody watching the live feed from home said,

423
00:26:48,220 --> 00:26:49,200
that looks like something.

424
00:26:51,410 --> 00:26:54,110
Taylor's own description of the moment is wonderfully undramatic.

425
00:26:54,650 --> 00:26:56,810
We filmed it because it was beautiful and unusual.

426
00:26:56,810 --> 00:27:00,990
And then we kind of descended back all the way down to the seafloor to do the exploration

427
00:27:00,990 --> 00:27:02,870
that the rest of that dive was focused on.

428
00:27:04,370 --> 00:27:05,430
They carried on with their day.

429
00:27:06,050 --> 00:27:09,370
And then they said nothing at all to anybody for five weeks.

430
00:27:11,170 --> 00:27:14,110
Chapter 14. Why they said nothing for five weeks.

431
00:27:16,470 --> 00:27:20,810
Because identifying a small transparent squid is genuinely hard and because of something

432
00:27:20,810 --> 00:27:22,230
that had happened two years earlier.

433
00:27:23,130 --> 00:27:27,090
In January 2023, a nonprofit called Colossal,

434
00:27:27,530 --> 00:27:28,890
founded by Matt Mulrennan,

435
00:27:29,270 --> 00:27:33,170
filmed a small glass squid roughly 680 miles south of Argentina.

436
00:27:34,550 --> 00:27:38,810
They described it as potentially the first Colossal squid ever filmed in its natural

437
00:27:38,810 --> 00:27:42,150
environment. The specialists split three ways.

438
00:27:42,770 --> 00:27:44,290
A juvenile Colossal squid,

439
00:27:45,090 --> 00:27:48,190
or an adult of a look -alike species called Galatuthi's glacial ass,

440
00:27:48,810 --> 00:27:50,510
or something not yet described at all.

441
00:27:51,550 --> 00:27:54,870
As of the end of 2025, it is still unresolved.

442
00:27:57,600 --> 00:28:01,140
So in March 2025, the team sent their footage to two people,

443
00:28:01,500 --> 00:28:02,900
independently, and waited.

444
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:07,360
Kat Bolstad at Auckland University of Technology,

445
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:11,180
and Aaron Evans, a specialist in exactly this family of squid.

446
00:28:12,740 --> 00:28:15,800
The same Aaron Evans who counted suckers at Te Papa in 2014,

447
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:17,960
and the same Aaron Evans who,

448
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:21,400
as a peer reviewer, had declined to confirm Colossal's footage.

449
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:28,420
His verdict on that one. The squid seen here could belong to different life stages of

450
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:30,720
either of those taxa. He would not call it.

451
00:28:32,040 --> 00:28:34,580
So when the same man looks at the 2025 footage,

452
00:28:35,140 --> 00:28:39,200
what changes his mind? The hooks,

453
00:28:39,680 --> 00:28:41,020
but not the ones on the tentacles.

454
00:28:43,810 --> 00:28:47,210
Every glass squid has hooks on the clubs at the end of its two long tentacles.

455
00:28:48,010 --> 00:28:52,930
That proves nothing. Bolstad's description of the test.

456
00:28:53,650 --> 00:28:57,430
The Colossal squid is the only squid that we know of that has suckers near the beak,

457
00:28:57,430 --> 00:28:59,250
then hooks midway along the arm,

458
00:28:59,590 --> 00:29:01,490
and then suckers again toward the arm tips.

459
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:06,400
Suckers, then hooks, then suckers.

460
00:29:06,700 --> 00:29:08,800
In that order, down the middle of the eight arms.

461
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:10,580
Out of every squid in the ocean,

462
00:29:11,020 --> 00:29:12,220
one animal is built that way.

463
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:17,480
It is there in the name Robson gave it in 1925,

464
00:29:17,940 --> 00:29:19,440
before anyone had seen a living one.

465
00:29:20,480 --> 00:29:22,360
Masonicotuthis. Middle claw squid.

466
00:29:25,060 --> 00:29:28,180
A hundred years later, that middle claw is what confirmed it.

467
00:29:31,280 --> 00:29:33,460
Chapter 15. What the footage didn't show.

468
00:29:33,460 --> 00:29:38,020
On the 15th of April, they released it.

469
00:29:38,680 --> 00:29:40,260
And what people saw was not a monster.

470
00:29:41,880 --> 00:29:44,560
A near -transparent animal about 30 cm long,

471
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:48,800
with iridescent eyes and visible internal organs drifting in the dark.

472
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:54,960
Scattered across that transparent body were rust red and brown chromatophores.

473
00:29:55,500 --> 00:29:57,920
Color cells. Which means even at this size,

474
00:29:58,040 --> 00:30:00,300
it can probably switch between transparent and opaque.

475
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:05,540
And for the first time, a colossal squid that was not damaged.

476
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:08,360
Steve O 'Shea, who was not part of the expedition,

477
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:09,940
put it as well as anyone.

478
00:30:11,340 --> 00:30:15,680
It is lovely to see this species being filmed by scientists in a non -invasive way.

479
00:30:16,080 --> 00:30:19,380
Alive rather than dead on a trawler deck or mangled in a net.

480
00:30:22,060 --> 00:30:23,540
Bolstad's framing was gentler still.

481
00:30:24,120 --> 00:30:28,440
It's exciting to see the first in situ footage of a juvenile colossal and humbling to

482
00:30:28,440 --> 00:30:30,420
think that they have no idea that humans exist.

483
00:30:32,820 --> 00:30:35,620
And she was explicit that she did not want the monster hype.

484
00:30:35,620 --> 00:30:40,120
We get to introduce the live colossal squid to the world as this beautiful,

485
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:45,520
little, delicate animal. Now the honest part.

486
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:47,420
What that footage did not do.

487
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:50,860
It showed no feeding. No hunting.

488
00:30:51,560 --> 00:30:56,100
No mating. Nothing about lifespan or growth or how an adult behaves.

489
00:30:58,720 --> 00:31:03,400
Evans, again, this is not the giant animal we'd expect the colossal squid to be.

490
00:31:03,840 --> 00:31:05,820
We could think of this maybe as a teenager squid.

491
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:07,760
It's still got a lot of growing to do.

492
00:31:08,900 --> 00:31:11,800
Even the 30 -centimeter figure is the team's estimate,

493
00:31:12,280 --> 00:31:13,500
not an independent measurement.

494
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:16,560
And one thing worth being precise about,

495
00:31:16,900 --> 00:31:20,240
because it matters, there is no peer -reviewed paper on this sighting.

496
00:31:21,740 --> 00:31:23,260
It was an institutional announcement,

497
00:31:23,740 --> 00:31:26,560
plus two named independent experts who confirmed it.

498
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:31,620
That is a strong chain. It is not the same thing as published research and it should

499
00:31:31,620 --> 00:31:32,920
not be described as such.

500
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:36,740
Chapter 16, Why It Stays Hidden.

501
00:31:38,700 --> 00:31:42,980
So why, after a century, is a juvenile the best we have managed?

502
00:31:44,600 --> 00:31:46,100
Bolstad's answer is not mystical.

503
00:31:46,740 --> 00:31:49,800
It is mechanical and it is slightly humiliating for us.

504
00:31:51,220 --> 00:31:53,960
Much of our scientific and filming gear is noisy and bright,

505
00:31:54,300 --> 00:31:58,060
so squid will be aware of our equipment long before we know they're there and they will

506
00:31:58,060 --> 00:32:03,070
stay well away. They know we are there long before we know

507
00:32:03,070 --> 00:32:07,960
they are there. Think about what that means alongside the eye.

508
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:10,340
Whether or not it evolved to spot a whale,

509
00:32:10,340 --> 00:32:15,200
an animal built to detect a faint moving glow at over a hundred metros in total darkness

510
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:19,300
is an animal that sees a floodlit robot coming from very far away indeed.

511
00:32:21,420 --> 00:32:24,960
We built the brightest thing in the deep ocean and then wondered why the shy animal never

512
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:29,970
turns up. Chapter 17, The Hunt

513
00:32:29,970 --> 00:32:34,280
Is Still Open. Which brings us to today.

514
00:32:35,740 --> 00:32:38,580
As of right now, there has been no second confirmed sighting.

515
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:41,620
No adult colossal squid has ever been filmed.

516
00:32:41,620 --> 00:32:45,620
The catalog of records still ends with that 30 -centimeter juvenile.

517
00:32:48,170 --> 00:32:52,090
The Schmidt team is building new camera systems aimed specifically at filming a grown

518
00:32:52,090 --> 00:32:55,070
one. But the Fall Corps, too,

519
00:32:55,390 --> 00:32:57,950
has no Southern Ocean expedition scheduled this year.

520
00:32:58,470 --> 00:33:01,070
Its entire 2026 program is in the Atlantic.

521
00:33:01,850 --> 00:33:05,850
Press reports at the time suggested it would not return to Antarctica until 2028,

522
00:33:06,110 --> 00:33:08,570
though no institutional document confirms that date.

523
00:33:09,890 --> 00:33:11,270
Colossal is still out there.

524
00:33:11,810 --> 00:33:14,570
Mulrennan's line, when the 2025 footage landed,

525
00:33:14,950 --> 00:33:17,310
was three words. The hunt continues.

526
00:33:18,830 --> 00:33:20,070
And there is one last thing,

527
00:33:20,430 --> 00:33:21,850
which I did not expect to find.

528
00:33:23,450 --> 00:33:27,970
In 2017, a review of everything known about this species ended with a recommendation.

529
00:33:29,050 --> 00:33:32,990
Bated cameras and remotely operated vehicles could be deployed in the search of live

530
00:33:32,990 --> 00:33:35,590
footage. Eight years later,

531
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:37,550
that is exactly what found it.

532
00:33:37,890 --> 00:33:40,510
Not a hunt. Not an expedition looking for it.

533
00:33:40,510 --> 00:33:42,470
A robot on its way somewhere else,

534
00:33:42,850 --> 00:33:44,730
and a stranger on the internet watching the feed.

535
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:50,360
I want to end on the thing that stayed with me while making this.

536
00:33:52,760 --> 00:33:56,140
There is an assumption, relayed in one review from one catalog,

537
00:33:56,620 --> 00:34:01,040
that the Southern Ocean might hold something like 90 million tons of colossal squid.

538
00:34:02,480 --> 00:34:03,880
Nobody has ever tested that.

